WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.240
Countries around the world are
struggling with what to do about

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.200
cryptocurrencies. Some countries want to
encourage them, others want to control

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.430
them, and others want them to just go
away. What are the concerns? Well, the UK

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:20.600
worries about price volatility and
criminal use. US regulators are talking

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.310
about fraud, unregulated activities, and
price bubbles. What appears to underlie

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:30.080
the push for regulation is a fear that
cryptocurrencies cannot be easily

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.250
controlled; that they may have impacts
that we can't foresee. The fears are

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.820
well-founded: Cryptocurrencies are
really just lines to computer code that

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.960
can be as variable as coders'
imaginations, and those pieces of code

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.579
are spread across computers around the
world. Yet, this is the future of digital

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:50.960
markets: People will be engaging in
economic relationships that are varied

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.609
and ever-changing. This is a difficult
future for government regulatory systems.

00:00:55.609 --> 00:00:59.179
They're used to regulating things like
bank accounts and stocks where

00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:03.829
specialized rules and organizations can
be set up. Cryptocurrencies could

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:06.800
disrupt this historical approach to
regulation.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.800
Do you think cryptocurrency should be regulated? If so, how?

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.460
Let us know your comments. Also, let us know what

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:18.880
other topics you'd like our scholars to
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:24.250
and subscribe for more research and
videos from AEI.

